WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.080
Hey, welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners episode 76 for June 19th, 2026.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:10.540
We've got some really cool AI stuff to talk about today. Let's dive in.

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:14.920
Picture this. You are working on a school project or a YouTube video,

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:19.720
and you hit a wall with editing software. Instead of digging through endless menus,

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:23.520
you just type what you want in plain English and the program handles it.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:29.980
That is exactly what Adobe just started testing with new AI assistants built right into Photoshop.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.820
Premiere, Illustrator, InDesign, and Frame.io.

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:39.480
Think of these assistants like a patient tutor who sits beside you and knows every hidden feature

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:45.460
of the app. You say something simple, such as make the background blur or remove the shaky footage

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:50.380
and the AI carries out the task inside the program you already use.

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:55.680
The assistants also keep track of what you worked on recently so they can pick up the thread

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:58.440
across different projects without needing reminders.

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:04.520
For anyone making posters, editing short videos, or designing graphics for the first time,

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:08.360
this removes the steep learning curve that used to stop people cold.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:13.860
It also makes professional design tools feel more approachable if you are thinking about future

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:19.480
careers in media or digital art. Right now, the feature is available in public beta.

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:25.040
If you have an Adobe Creative Cloud account, you can open one of the apps and look for the new

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:31.440
AI Assistant panel to join the test. If you do not have an account yet, the free web versions of

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:36.980
Adobe Express already include similar chat style AI tools. You can try right away.

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:42.980
Okay, now for my favorite part of the show. The deep dive where we look under the hood

00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:50.160
at how one piece of AI technology actually works. Today we are unpacking how researchers teach AI

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:57.040
models to stay helpful and safe even when no one is watching. Imagine you are training a dog to sit

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:03.000
politely in the living room. You reward it every time it stays calm around guests and after a while,

00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:08.580
the dog starts behaving politely in the kitchen and the backyard too. That is close to what

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:14.680
researchers are doing with AI models. They first show the model thousands of examples of truthful

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:20.760
and careful answers in one narrow area such as health questions. Every time the model stays

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:26.940
accurate and avoids harm, it receives a reward signal. Next, they test whether that careful habit

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:32.720
appears in completely different subjects the model was never trained on, such as mass problems or

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:38.840
story writing. Surprisingly, the good behavior often carries over. The training focuses on the

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:44.240
underlying pattern of being helpful without lying rather than on specific words or topics.

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:50.840
And that is basically how alignment training works. Not so scary once you see it as an upgraded

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:56.900
version of your phone's auto complete learning your personal style. Now, let's look at some cool

00:02:56.900 --> 00:03:02.320
stuff you can try yourself. A free website called In the Wates lets you search any person's name

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:08.660
and see how strongly different AI models remember them from their training data. It shows a simple

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:14.100
strength score so you can compare, for example, whether a famous musician is remembered more deeply

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:20.840
than a lesser known athlete. This is useful because it shows that AI is not magic. It literally

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:27.280
stores patterns from the internet it was trained on. Go to the site, type in a name like Mozart or

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:33.180
your favorite YouTuber and look at the score. You can even compare two names side by side to see

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:38.880
whose information sits more deeply in the model. Another quick experiment comes from the Adobe

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:45.560
tools we talked about earlier. Head to the free Adobe Express website, open a photo or short video

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:52.680
and type a request such as Brighton the Sky or add text that matches the mood. Watch how the AI

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:58.800
suggests edits without you needing to learn any menu commands. Notice how the suggestions feel

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:04.640
like they are learning from the style of your project as you go. Time for quick bits, the rapid

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:10.700
fire section where we share smaller stories that caught our eye. Anthropic recently shared videos

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:17.180
of robot dogs being programmed by their Claude model to pick up objects. The AI wrote the instructions

00:04:17.180 --> 00:04:23.440
much faster than human teams did last year. The dogs still need practice even after the instructions

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:29.560
are generated. Claude produced the movement code for the robot dogs. The videos show the dogs

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:35.760
attempting to grasp items after receiving the AI written commands. This gives a peek at AI helping

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:42.380
control real robots around the house or in schools. The source for the videos is an anthropic post on

00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:49.200
X. In another update, researchers at OpenAI trained models to be truthful and helpful in one narrow

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:56.100
area and then checked whether that behavior appeared in unrelated tasks. The training used examples

00:04:56.100 --> 00:05:02.660
focused on staying accurate and avoiding harm. Tests then moved to subjects the models had not seen

00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:08.760
during that phase. Early results show the models became harder to trick into saying harmful things.

00:05:09.460 --> 00:05:15.000
The models continued answering normal questions without change. The effort aims to make future

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:22.900
chatbots safer for everyone to use. The source for the update is an OpenAI post on X and that's a wrap

